  "name": null,
  "problem": "Find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942110/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{csc}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=2{(-2)}^{2}=8.\n$$",
